Z||000000||1FieroGTRacingInc||03-10-2000||09:08 AM||||What is the MOST HP you can get from the 2.8? I mean THE MOST HP you can SQUEEZE out of one....   Thank's ... ||||reg||6
Z||000001||Old Lar||03-10-2000||10:33 AM||||How much money do you have to spend??<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/rolleyes.gif"><BR>Nascar gets 700-800 horses out of a ~350-358 block, a 2.8 is about 170 cu in.  What maybe 300-400 horses for about 10 minutes before the engine goes. The 2.8 wasn't built for hp.<P>Just buy a NASCAR engine for around $30000, then get John Norman or V8 Archie to put that engine in your car.<P>You'll need some sort of transmission for the car, figure a few grand for that. Upgrade the brakes so that you can stop.<P>I figure if you have $50K you'll have the fastest Fiero that you won't be able to drive on the street.<p>[This message has been edited by Old Lar (edited 03-10-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000002||Phaeton||03-10-2000||11:07 AM||||GM part # 10051141 is an aluminum racing block offered through chevrolet. It has engine mounts for transverse or longitudinal installation and the starter can go either side.  It is rated for a sustained three hundred horsepower output.  Power can be traded for duty cycle.  ||||reg||1
Z||000003||1FST2M6||03-10-2000||12:00 PM||||200HP is easily attainable without any over-bore or head mods. Swaping the cam, raise the compressions and your there... The factory only clained our cars to have 140hp and 170lbft at the flywheel. Like most GM spec i think they undercut our numbers for either insurance purposes or consumer reasons i'm not aware of.  Add 27.5% to these numbers for the actuall flywheel numbers. The blue is without nitrous, the red is with nitrous. And 96,000 miles on the original block and heads.  <A HREF="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/2m6dyno.jpg" TARGET=_blank>www.fiero.nl/uploads/2m6dyno.jpg</A> ||||reg||1
Z||000004||batboy||03-10-2000||12:06 PM||||Sometimes I get tired of preaching this, but if you're talking about a pushrod and hydraulic lifters engine, forget horsepower because you can't crank up the RPM like the overhead cam engines. Besides, the 2.8 won't last at high RPM anyway. Concentrate on building massive amounts of mid-range torque. Trouble is, the 2.8 is a small displacement engine, so you can only get so much out of it. If you're willing to spend several grand on a racing 2.8 engine, why not swap in something that will give you the power you want and that will be reliable in the long run.||||reg||1
Z||000005||WKDFIRO||03-10-2000||12:21 PM||||I would have to respectfully say that 27% seems a little high for a conversion despite what the book would tell you. Just using the at the wheel numbers should be enough. Those are concrete. The rest is guessing at best unless you put it on a specific engine dyno.<P>As always, I reserve the right to be completely totally wrong!<P><p>[This message has been edited by WKDFIRO (edited 03-10-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000006||87SC/GT||03-10-2000||12:43 PM||||I have been following Curtis Walkers 3.1 Turbo build up. He tells me he is expecting 488 hp @ 16 psi boost and 628hp @ 25 psi boost if the heads will stay on it long enough.He hit 315hp @8 psi on the dyno before  his clutch said good bye. Check out his page. It is a very  impressive project.<P> <A HREF="http://members.tripod.com/TurboZ24" TARGET=_blank>http://members.tripod.com/TurboZ24</A> ||||reg||1
